Microsoft Learn: Describe Core Architectural Components of Azure

Platform: Microsoft Learn
Module: Describe Core Architectural Components of Azure
Track: AZ-104 Microsoft Azure Administrator
Difficulty: Beginner to Intermediate
Date: 2026

Objective

Learn the foundational architectural components of Microsoft Azure, including Azure’s physical infrastructure, geographic organization, and management hierarchy.

This module focused on understanding how Azure organizes cloud infrastructure through datacenters, regions, availability zones, resource groups, subscriptions, and management groups to provide scalable, highly available, and manageable cloud environments.

Step 1: Understanding Azure Datacenters

The module introduced Azure datacenters as the physical foundation of Microsoft’s global cloud infrastructure.

Azure datacenters are secure physical facilities that contain:

Servers
Networking equipment
Storage systems
Cooling systems
Power infrastructure

Key characteristics:

High physical security
Redundant power systems
Advanced cooling infrastructure
High-speed networking
Disaster recovery protections

The module emphasized that Azure services run inside these globally distributed datacenters.

Step 2: Understanding Azure Regions

The training covered Azure regions and their role in global cloud deployment.

An Azure region is a geographic area containing one or more datacenters connected through low-latency networking.

Examples:

East US
West Europe
Canada Central
Southeast Asia

Key benefits of regions:

Reduced latency for nearby users
Compliance with data residency laws
Disaster recovery support
Geographic scalability

Key concepts learned:

Applications should be deployed near users whenever possible
Different regions may provide different service availability
Region selection affects performance and compliance

The module demonstrated how Azure regions support global cloud architectures.

Step 3: Region Pairs and Sovereign Regions

A major focus of the module was Azure region pairing.

Region Pairs

Azure pairs regions within the same geography to support disaster recovery and business continuity.

Examples:

Primary Region	Paired Region
East US	West US
North Europe	West Europe

Benefits of region pairs:

Automatic replication support
Improved disaster recovery
Sequential Azure updates
Priority recovery during outages
Sovereign Regions

The module also introduced sovereign regions designed for government and regulatory requirements.

Examples:

Azure Government
Azure China
Azure Germany

Key concepts learned:

Sovereign regions operate independently from the global Azure cloud
Designed for compliance, legal, and regulatory requirements
Often used by government agencies and regulated industries
Step 4: Understanding Availability Zones

The module introduced Availability Zones as a high-availability feature inside Azure regions.

Availability Zones are physically separate datacenters within the same region.

Characteristics:

Independent power
Independent cooling
Independent networking

Benefits:

Protection against datacenter failure
Higher application availability
Improved resiliency

Key concepts learned:

Availability Zones improve fault tolerance
Applications can be distributed across zones
Zone-redundant services remain operational during failures

The module emphasized Availability Zones as a critical component of highly available cloud architectures.

Step 5: Understanding Azure Resources

The training introduced Azure resources as the building blocks of Azure services.

Examples of Azure resources:

Virtual Machines
Storage Accounts
Virtual Networks
Databases
Load Balancers

Key concepts learned:

Every Azure service is deployed as a resource
Resources are managed through Azure Resource Manager
Resources can be grouped and managed together

Azure resources form the core infrastructure deployed by Azure Administrators.

Step 6: Resource Groups

The module covered Resource Groups as logical containers for Azure resources.

Resource Groups help organize related resources together.

Example:

RG-WebApp
├── Virtual Machine
├── Storage Account
├── Virtual Network
└── Database

Benefits:

Simplified management
Easier permission assignment
Centralized monitoring
Group-based lifecycle management

Key lessons learned:

Resources in a Resource Group often share the same lifecycle
Deleting a Resource Group deletes all contained resources
Resource Groups simplify administration
Step 7: Understanding Subscriptions

The module introduced Azure subscriptions as management and billing boundaries.

Subscriptions provide:

Billing separation
Access control boundaries
Resource quotas
Policy application scope

Examples:

Production Subscription
Development Subscription
Testing Subscription

Key concepts learned:

Organizations commonly use multiple subscriptions
Permissions can be assigned at the subscription level
Subscriptions help separate environments and costs
Step 8: Understanding Management Groups

The module introduced Management Groups for large-scale governance.

Management Groups allow organizations to organize and manage multiple subscriptions together.

Benefits:

Centralized governance
Policy inheritance
RBAC inheritance
Enterprise-wide administration

Example hierarchy:

Management Group
    └── Subscription
            └── Resource Group
                    └── Resources

Key lessons learned:

Policies applied at higher levels inherit downward
Management Groups simplify enterprise governance
Large organizations commonly use Management Groups
Step 9: Azure Hierarchy

The module emphasized Azure’s management hierarchy.

Hierarchy structure:

Level	Purpose
Management Groups	Govern multiple subscriptions
Subscriptions	Billing and access boundaries
Resource Groups	Organize related resources
Resources	Actual Azure services

Key concepts learned:

Access and policies inherit downward
Proper hierarchy improves governance
Azure environments are easier to manage through structured organization
Step 10: Real-World Scenario

The learning scenario focused on organizations deploying workloads globally while maintaining governance, high availability, and centralized management.

Administrative goals included:

Organizing cloud resources efficiently
Supporting disaster recovery
Improving application availability
Managing costs and permissions
Applying centralized governance policies

This demonstrated how Azure’s architectural structure supports enterprise-scale cloud environments.

